Swapping via Pointers
Swap 1 — a=b:
cint a, b;
a = b;    // a gets value of b (loses original a)
Swap 2 — temp method:
cint a = 5, b = 9;
int x;
x = a;    // x=5
a = b;    // a=9
b = x;    // b=5
// Result: a=9, b=5  ✓
Swap 3 — XOR method (no temp):
cint a = 5, b = 9;
a = a ^ b;    // a = 12
b = a ^ b;    // b = 5
a = a ^ b;    // a = 9
Swap 4 — arithmetic (a+b):
ca = a + b;   // a=14
b = a - b;   // b=5
a = a - b;   // a=9

15. Call by Value vs Call by Address
Call by Value

Passes a copy of the value to the function.
Changes inside function do not affect original variables.

cvoid swap(int x, int y) {
    int t = x;
    x = y;
    y = t;
}

void main() {
    int a = 5, b = 2;
    swap(a, b);
    printf("%d %d", a, b);  // 5  2  (unchanged)
}
Memory:
swap:  x[5]  y[2]  t[5] → x=2, y=5 (local copies only)
main:  a[5]  b[2]        → unchanged
Examples of call by value:
ca = 4;
printf("%d", sqrt(a));   // 2 (sqrt gets copy)
printf("%d", a);         // 4 (original unchanged)

b = -5;
printf("%d", abs(b));    // 5
printf("%d", b);         // -5

Call by Address

Passes the address of variables.
Changes inside function directly affect original variables.
scanf() uses call by address.

cvoid swap(int *p1, int *p2) {
    int t = *p1;
    *p1 = *p2;
    *p2 = t;
}

void main() {
    int a = 5, b = 2;
    swap(&a, &b);
    printf("%d %d", a, b);  // 2  5  (swapped!)
}
Memory:
main: a[5]@500  b[2]@600
swap: p1=500, p2=600
      t = *p1 = 5
      *p1 = *p2 → a becomes 2
      *p2 = t   → b becomes 5

16. Arrays
Why Arrays?
Problem with individual variables:
cint id1, id2, id3, ... id10;   // pain to write all names
// Also: random memory allocation (not contiguous)
Solution — Arrays:

Same type variables → use arrays
Same job variables → use arrays

Definition

Array = indexed collection of homogeneous elements stored in continuously allocated (contiguous) memory.

cint a[5];   // a is an array of 5 integer elements
            // OR: integer array of size 5
Uses [] — Index/Subscript/Binary Operator
cint a, b, c, d, e;
// ↓ equivalent to:
int a[5];   // a[0], a[1], a[2], a[3], a[4]
Memory layout:
a[0] a[1] a[2] a[3] a[4]
500  502  504  506  508    (addresses, 2 bytes each for int)

Valid & Invalid Array Definitions
c// Valid:
1) int a[5];
2) int a[5];    // (same)
3) Array name need → Valid

// Invalid:
int a[6];         // CE if access beyond 5 elements
char c[3000];     // Valid (just memory)
int a[0];         // Invalid — CE (zero size)
int a[2.5];       // Invalid — CE (float size)
float f[5000];    // Valid (Turbo C limit ~65535 addresses)
int int x = 10;   // CE
int a[x];         // in C language — variable as size is NOT allowed
                  // (in old compilers; new compilers may allow)

Array Size Rules

Array — when we need n variables of same type, doing the same job → use array.
Same scenario — when we need to go for arrays.
Arrays are indexed collection of homogeneous elements, stored at a fixed location in contiguous memory.


In Turbo C, there is an availability of 65535 continuous addresses. Where as in new compilers, there is no information of how many continuous addresses — it must be a constant expression (old compilers).


In old C language, there is no checking of boundary. In new C language variables are also allowed (VLA — Variable Length Arrays in C99+).


